A formalization gap is mathematics supplied or used by the source whose
Litex proof remains unfinished.  A source-deferred proof is an unchecked
theorem whose source explicitly says `Exercise`, `why?`, or `How?`; it is a
visible trust boundary, not an omitted source proof.  Each item states the
missing mathematics and the source-facing result it supports.

Chapter 4 — Integers and rationals (2 direct trust sites)

Formalization gaps (2)

Item 04-01 — Section 4.3; rational_closeness_of_products.
Supply the three-term product-error estimate obtained from
xz-yw=(x-y)z+x(z-w)-(x-y)(z-w), using multiplicativity and the triangle
inequality for absolute value.  This gives the epsilon-close bound for two
simultaneously moving rational factors.

Item 04-02 — Section 4.4; rational_has_positive_denominator_representation.
Construct a positive-denominator representation from an arbitrary nonzero
integer denominator by replacing the denominator by its absolute value and,
in the negative case, negating the numerator.  This representation supplies
the Euclidean-quotient proof of the integer interval containing a rational.

